msgid "Lay out the matrix"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Let the layout engine arrange the matrix by epochs, and keep your own "
"order.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Matrix draws each epoch as a horizontal band, the most recent at the "
"top, and places the units inside their band so that a relation runs from "
"the later unit down to the earlier one. The arrangement is stored in the "
"study, with the places you give the units by hand. Nothing moves a node "
"by itself: the layout runs when you ask for it, and every layout is one "
"step of ``Edit ▸ Undo``."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When you open a study, EMStudio draws it from the arrangement stored in "
"it and writes nothing. A study read from a GraphML, or a study whose "
"graph carries no arrangement yet, is laid out once on opening."
msgstr ""

msgid "Arrange the whole matrix"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Click the **Layout** icon on the bar of the Graph window (its tooltip "
"says **Arranges this view again; one undo takes it back.**). The same "
"command is ``⋯ ▸ Layout ▸ Layout`` in the window's menu and **Reorder "
"everything** at the foot of the menu that a right click on the empty "
"canvas opens."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The message **Layout from the hand's arrangement** confirms it. The "
"engine starts from the order the units already have, yours included, and "
"arranges them again by epochs."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Hold :kbd:`⌥` (:kbd:`Alt`) while you click the icon for a fresh "
"arrangement that leaves out the places given by hand: the message is "
"**New layout**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The layout always arranges the whole graph, also when a filter hides part"
" of it (see :doc:`filter-the-graph`), so the study you reopen shows what "
"you saw after the layout. A filter alone hides and shows nodes without "
"moving the others: a hidden node leaves its place empty until the next "
"layout."
msgstr ""

msgid "Reduce the crossings"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Choose ``⋯ ▸ Layout ▸ Layout reducing the crossings`` in the window's "
"menu, or the same entry from a right click on the empty canvas."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The engine reorders the units within their bands to cross fewer lines. "
"The message gives the count before and after, for example **Laid out "
"reducing the crossings: 146 → 86 — one undo takes it back**. When it "
"finds no better order, it says **No crossing fewer** and lays out as "
"**Layout** does."
msgstr ""

msgid "Keep your own order"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Drag a unit* where you want it inside its band. At the next layout it "
"keeps its place beside the nearest unit. This is the setting **«Layout» "
"respects where I drop the nodes**, in ``Settings… ▸ General ▸ Matrix · "
"layout``, on by default; turned off, a dropped unit only shifts its "
"stored place by the same amount."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Lock a unit*: right-click it and choose **Lock position (Layout keeps it"
" here)**. The layout leaves it where it is and arranges the rest around "
"it. **Unlock position** gives it back."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Reorder one unit*: **Reorder this node** forgets the place you gave it, "
"and the layout puts it back, leaving every other unit where it is. A "
"locked unit keeps its lock."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Reorder one band*: right-click the name of an epoch at the left edge of "
"the Matrix and choose **Reorder the lane**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Groups** stay where you drag them, and **Reorder the group** frees one;"
" see :doc:`groups-and-folding`. **Reorder everything** frees every group "
"you placed, and keeps the locks of the units."
msgstr ""

msgid "Change the epoch of a unit"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Drag a unit into another band and release it: the unit now belongs to "
"that epoch, and the Matrix places it in the new band without moving the "
"others. Press :kbd:`M` with units selected for **Move to epoch…**, or "
":kbd:`⌥M` (:kbd:`Alt+M`) for **Extend to epoch…**, to do the same for a "
"whole selection (see :doc:`edit-many-units`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A node with no epoch of its own is drawn in the band of the nodes its "
"relations come from, inside a dotted grey frame. Its tooltip says **No "
"epoch of its own: drawn in the lane of the nodes its edges come from "
"(dotted frame). Give it an epoch to place it in time.**"
msgstr ""

msgid "In Graph mode"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **Graph** mode of the same window draws the nodes as discs, with no "
"bands. ``⋯ ▸ Layout`` offers three arrangements: **Layered**, **Radial** "
"and **Force**. A disc you drag stays where you leave it; these places are"
" kept on this computer, for each graph, and not in the study. The "
"**Layout** icon forgets them and arranges the graph again."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A Matrix arranged by epochs, with the order you gave by hand kept, and "
"saved with the study."
msgstr ""

